<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <GenerateProgramFile>false</GenerateProgramFile>

    <!-- Package Metadata -->
    <Version>1.4.15</Version>
    <PackageId>FSharp.Azure.Quantum</PackageId>
    <Title>FSharp.Azure.Quantum</Title>
    <Description>F# library for Azure Quantum with quantum-first optimization (Graph Coloring, TSP, MaxCut, Knapsack, Network Flow, Portfolio), Azure Quantum backend integration (IonQ, Rigetti, Quantinuum, Atom Computing), extensible circuit validation, local quantum simulation, QAOA with automatic parameter optimization (Nelder-Mead), OpenQASM 2.0 support, error mitigation techniques (ZNE, PEC, REM), Quantum Random Number Generation (QRNG), and educational quantum algorithms (Grover, QFT, Amplitude Amplification, QPE, Shor, HHL) and quantum chemistry (VQE, QPE) with cloud backend execution as whole-circuit jobs. Native IQuantumBackend integration for IonQ, Rigetti, Quantinuum, Atom Computing, IQM and D-Wave, plus Pasqal neutral-atom analog hardware (via Pulser) and a neutral-atom analog programming model; additional platforms (IBM, Amazon Braket, Google Cirq) are targetable via OpenQASM 2.0 export/import.</Description>
    <Summary>F# library for Azure Quantum with quantum-first optimization, quantum backends (IonQ, Rigetti, Quantinuum, Atom Computing), QAOA with automatic parameter tuning, QRNG, and educational algorithms with cloud execution</Summary>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>v1.4.15: cloud results keep their recorded shots; a local Azure Quantum service; honest error bars

- FIXED (cloud results; the 1.4.14 known limitation): a cloud backend's returned state carries the job's recorded counts (QuantumState.withRecordedCounts, tryRecordedCounts, recordedShotCount, recordedShots; UnifiedBackend.recordedShots, recordedShotCount). QuantumState.measure and UnifiedBackend.measureState on such a state return the job's own shots, drawn without replacement and never more than it measured, instead of drawing new outcomes from its histogram with a pseudo-random generator. The array can be shorter than requested: Grover, Bernstein–Vazirani, Deutsch–Jozsa and VQC take frequencies over the shots measured (Grover reported 1,000 measurements from a 500-shot job). MeasurementHistogram states behave the same way, and Braket results carry their counts too
- CHANGED (cloud results): a job's recorded shots come in an order seeded from the counts themselves, so a run against a seeded simulator (LocalQuantumService Seed) repeats exactly; the order was taken from process-wide randomness
- FIXED (error mitigation): the strategy selected for a high accuracy requirement had a fallback with a ZNE component, which applyStrategy refuses on a finished histogram like the primary, so applying the recommended strategy was always an Error; the fallback is readout correction alone
- FIXED (whole-circuit trials): a job whose histogram keys are narrower than the packed circuit (unpadded legacy result payloads) is widened with zeros instead of throwing from WholeCircuit.runTrials
- CHANGED (drone examples): the four SITL pictures were re-flown in ArduPilot 4.7.1 at --speedup 1 and their README result tables updated; examples/Drones/SITL.md notes the build-from-source route and that SITL runs about 0.1-0.2% fast at --speedup 2 and 3
- FIXED (Shor): each period-finding attempt reads a distinct measured shot. On a cloud backend the attempts walk the job's recorded shots one by one and a further job is submitted only when all of them have been tried; they used to re-read the one finished job, so a one-shot backend spent all 16 attempts on a single outcome. A 100-shot backend covers 16 attempts with one job
- FIXED (Shor): periods come from the continued-fraction convergents of the measured outcome (and their multiples below N), the smallest r with a^r ≡ 1 (mod N). The old post-processing picked the fraction with denominator up to N closest to the phase, which is not a convergent: with the 6 counting qubits that fit N = 21 the outcome 11/64 matched 3/17 instead of 1/6, so the period of 2 mod 21 was almost never found and Shor.factor21 failed
- CHANGED (BB84, E91): the whole-circuit route groups trials by kind (BB84: Alice's bit and basis, Eve's basis, Bob's basis; E91: the three bases) and runs each kind in slots side by side on disjoint qubits, so every shot of every job is used (new WholeCircuit.runTrials, TrialKind, trialWidth, MaxTrialWidth = 16). A 200-bit BB84 key on a 100-shot backend takes 1 job (was 68) and at most 2 with Eve (was 135); E91 over 600 pairs takes 2 jobs (was 100) and at most 9 with Eve. With one shot per job the job count is as before
- FIXED (BB84, E91): circuits are packed to the backend's runnable width instead of a fixed 12 qubits, so both run on the 8-qubit density-matrix simulator. NoisyLocalBackend now declares its 8-qubit limit (IQubitLimitedBackend)
- FIXED (E91): on the gate-by-gate route Eve measured Alice's and Bob's qubits as two independent samples of the Bell state; it is now one joint measurement, so her two outcomes agree as they must
- CHANGED (teleportation): the whole-circuit route runs the Z, X and Y tomography settings as three copies of the protocol side by side in one 9-qubit circuit: one job per call instead of three. BobState is the Z copy's 3-qubit state
- FIXED (whole-circuit oracles): an oracle the whole-circuit route cannot read is always an Error. An unknown closure used to be probed with an empty state and treated as the identity when it returned that state unchanged, so a closure that branches on its input could be submitted as no gates and a balanced oracle reported constant. Oracles that carry their gates are the new WholeCircuit.GateOracle (built by gateOracle, and the new identityOracle), recognised by type; DeutschJozsa.constantZeroOracle and constantOneOracle are identityOracle
- ADDED: CircuitBuilder.mapQubits and WholeCircuit.shiftOps place a gate list on other qubits
- ADDED (LocalQuantumService): an optional local HTTP service that emulates the Azure Quantum workspace REST API (job submit, status, cancel, list, SAS blob storage, bearer-token checks) and runs each job on the local simulator, so the cloud backends' full Azure path — transpilation, IonQ JSON, Quil and OpenQASM serialisation, job lifecycle, result download and parsing — can be tested without sending data to Azure. IonQ, Rigetti, Quantinuum, IQM and Atom Computing targets are supported; programs it cannot decode fail as the provider would; failures and rejected submissions can be injected. LocalQuantumService.start, CreateHttpClient, WorkspaceUrl, Jobs, Requests (docs/local-quantum-service.md)
- FIXED (Rigetti, Quantinuum, IQM, Atom Computing): circuits were submitted without measurements — Quil declared ro but never MEASUREd into it, and OpenQASM for a unitary circuit had no measure — so a real provider would have read out nothing. A circuit that measures no qubit is now measured in full at the end (CloudBackendHelpers.withTerminalMeasurements); CircuitBuilder.Measure converts to MEASURE q ro[q]
- FIXED (Rigetti, Quantinuum results): RigettiBackend.parseRigettiResults reads Azure's per-shot ro register (rigetti.quil-results.v1) and QuantinuumBackend.parseQuantinuumResult the per-shot c register (honeywell.quantum-results.v1); both read only an aggregated histogram before. The aggregated shapes are still accepted
- ADDED (tests): end-to-end tests run Grover, Shor, BB84, E91, teleportation, the oracle protocols, QRNG, Primitives and job budgets through the real cloud backend classes against LocalQuantumService, pinning each answer and the number of jobs submitted
- FIXED (quantum Monte Carlo): QuantumMonteCarlo.estimateExpectation reports the Cramér–Rao standard error of the maximum-likelihood amplitude-estimation fit (≈0.0013 for a = 0.25 at 1,000 shots) instead of 1/GroverIterations (0.25). The fit is solved to the exact maximum instead of stopping at its 1.6e-5 rad grid, so exact-simulator estimates are exact to about 1e-8
- ADDED (quantum Monte Carlo, option pricing): amplitude estimation reads a topological backend's logical-qubit probabilities, so QMC, option pricing and the risk engine run on topological backends (a European call prices at the simulator's value on the Ising backend)
- CHANGED (option pricing, different results): the price grid uses the conditional mean of each equal-probability bin (new StatisticalDistributions.discretizeLogNormalBinMeans) instead of the mid-bin quantile, which keeps E[S] exact. With the fit fix, 6-qubit prices on the exact simulator match Black–Scholes to about 0.04% (were 0.4–1.7% low), Vega to 0.1% (was −1.3%) and Rho to 2.5% or better (was up to 5.6%); Gamma is limited by the price grid (8 qubits: 0.06%, was −4.6%)
- CHANGED (ADAPT-VQE, ADAPT-QAOA): on shot-sampling (cloud) backends a run is capped by the new MaxCloudJobs config field (default Some 2000; None = no cap). A run whose first operator or layer cannot fit is refused with a MaxCloudJobs ValidationError before any job; otherwise it stops before an iteration that could cross the cap and returns the best ansatz so far with JobCapReached = true. estimateCloudJobs gives the plan (a 3-qubit toy submitted 9,730 jobs uncapped). Results add EnergyStandardError (None on exact backends) and CloudJobs. solveQubo keeps one job of the cap for its final sample
- CHANGED (quantum kernels): on a shot-sampling backend, a shots argument different from the backend's Shots is an Error naming both (as Primitives.sample), returned before any job; it was silently ignored. This also applies to SimilaritySearch, AnomalyDetection and QuantumKernelSVM on cloud backends. Exact simulators still sample shots
- ADDED (HHL): HHL.executeWithRelativePhases recovers the signs and relative phases of the solution on whole-circuit (cloud) backends with interference circuits: one Hadamard circuit per solution qubit (plus one RX(π/2) circuit per qubit for complex systems), 1 + log₂N jobs for a real system. New readout HhlReadout.MeasuredRelativePhases (a new union case). On backends with state access it equals HHL.execute
- CHANGED (HHL regression): QuantumRegressionHHL.train works on cloud backends: it measures the weights' signs with executeWithRelativePhases (1 + log₂ of the padded dimension jobs per fit, e.g. 3 for 4 weights) and recovers the scale by the same least-squares fit as on the simulator; it returned an Error there before. The simulator path is unchanged. New field RegressionResult.Circuits

v1.4.14: QAOA runs forwards and minimises; chemistry VQE runs real circuits; graph coloring, scheduling, portfolio and QUBO fixes

- FIXED (every QAOA solver): CircuitAbstraction.qaoaCircuitToCircuit stored gates in program order while every consumer reads Circuit.Gates most-recent-first, so LocalBackend, NoisyBackend, the IonQ/Rigetti/Braket/Topological converters and the OpenQASM export ran each QAOA circuit backwards (mixers first, Hadamards last). Gates are now stored most-recent-first; CircuitBuilder.multiGate had the same fault
- FIXED (every QAOA solver): the standard mixer was +ΣX, so positive γ with β in (0, π/4) raised the objective while every solver minimises. The mixer is now −ΣX (RX(−2β)), QaoaSimulator included. With both fixes the default QAOA configuration beats uniform sampling on every solver tested, e.g. probability of the optimum 8× uniform for MaxCut, 15× for graph coloring, 82× for a 4-city TSP
- CHANGED: the shared QAOA circuit builder normalises the cost Hamiltonian (largest Ising coefficient 1, new ProblemHamiltonian.normalize), so default angles are meaningful on large penalty QUBOs. Returned angles refer to the normalised Hamiltonian; energies and costs stay in original units
- CHANGED: QaoaExecutionHelpers grid search and Nelder–Mead rank angles by expected QUBO energy (exact when the backend exposes amplitudes, otherwise the sample mean) instead of the best single sample, and Nelder–Mead starts from a ramp instead of a random point. The TSP solver uses this shared objective with bounds [0, π] × [0, π/2]
- CHANGED (Grover): with an unknown number of solutions (predicate and combined oracles) Grover.search tries the iteration counts for M = N, N/2, ..., 1 and stops at the first run with a verified solution, instead of assuming a single solution and over-rotating; since every candidate is verified against the oracle, outcomes are kept down to a uniform draw's frequency (1/N)
- CHANGED (ConstraintScheduler): the silent greedy classical fallback for small problems is removed. Cost goals use quantum minimum finding (Dürr–Høyer: Grover searches with a lowered cost bound until none is found). Without MaxBudget the cost bound is tasks × the most expensive resource; it was the sum of resource costs, which excluded every schedule when tasks outnumber resources
- CHANGED (graph coloring): ConflictPenalty, AvoidColors, MaxColors, Objective (MinimizeColors, BalanceColors, MinimizeConflicts) and Priority take effect; they were stored and ignored. Soft terms are bounded in total below the hard-constraint penalty, so the QUBO minimum is a valid coloring whenever one exists (ConflictWeight above 0.5). A conflict listed from both ends is one edge. A graph with no edges is colored without a circuit and reported as such (IsQuantum false; HybridSolver Method Classical). Added ColoringPreferences, toQuboWithPreferences, solveWithPreferencesAsync; Cost and BestEnergy report the QUBO energy
- FIXED (QUBO): ProblemTransformer.encodeTspEdgeBased put −distance on the diagonal, rewarding the longest edges, and lacked the linear one-hot terms; it is now a one-hot encoding (subtours are not excluded; the penalty must exceed n × the largest distance). encodeVariablesWithConstraints, encodeVariablesWithCustomConstraints and VariableEncoding.constraintPenalty (OneHot) doubled their pair terms; all use the symmetric convention now
- ADDED (portfolio): optional covariance: PortfolioProblem.Covariance, createProblemWithCovariance, createProblemWithCorrelation, validateCovariance, covarianceFromCorrelation, portfolioRisk, HybridSolver.solvePortfolioWithCovariance, QuantumPortfolioSolver.solveWithCovarianceAsync and a C# overload. Risk is sqrt(wᵀΣw) when a covariance is given
- CHANGED (portfolio, different results): the quantum solver uses a lot model minimising −μᵀw + λwᵀΣw, searches p = 1 angles on a small grid, returns the best feasible sample (MinHolding, MaxHolding, budget) and skips assets whose price exceeds one lot; part of the budget can stay uninvested. The classical greedy selection is unchanged but reports covariance risk. Transaction costs are in budget fractions
- ADDED (task scheduling): Resource.AvailableWindows (minutes from schedule start, bounds inclusive) and task EarliestStart are honoured by ClassicalSolver.solve and solveQuantum (forbidden slots penalised and cleared, decoded schedules re-checked, unplaceable tasks refused before any circuit runs); Priority breaks ties in solveQuantum. Windows beyond the representable time range return an Error
- CHANGED (chemistry, different results): VQE.run ran no circuit for H2, H2O and LiH and returned tabulated energies; it now runs UCCSD-VQE with BFGS and reaches FCI for H2 at every bond length. SolverConfig.IntegralProvider was never read; it is now used. Integrals come from the provider, from computed STO-3G or 6-31G integrals for H/He molecules (Sto3gIntegrals; multi-start RHF with DIIS and stability following), or the call returns an Error; a tabulated number is never labelled VQE. Results carry EnergySource, EnergyEstimation, ErrorMitigationApplied and Notes
- ADDED (chemistry): MoleculeFormats.FciDump reads the integrals (it read the header only), and FciDumpIntegrals exposes FCIDUMP files from PySCF, Psi4, Molpro or OpenMolcas as an IntegralProvider. The chemistry builder gains integralProvider and a working molecule_from_fcidump. UCCSD-VQE runs on whole-circuit (cloud) backends by sampling qubit-wise commuting measurement groups with SPSA (new IShotSamplingBackend)
- CHANGED (chemistry): spin states other than closed shells and one-electron doublets, ansätze above 64 parameters (VQE.MaxUccsdParameters) and bases other than STO-3G and 6-31G for computed integrals return an Error before any work. ClassicalDFT is deterministic and returns an Error outside its tabulated states. Molecule.nuclearRepulsion sums all atom pairs in bohr. Gradient failures surface as an Error
- FIXED (error mitigation): readout mitigation on the VQE path read histogram keys in the wrong qubit order and dropped negative quasi-probabilities, biasing energies. ZNE and PEC on histograms return an Error instead of reporting a readout-only correction as applied
- FIXED (tree search): quantumTreeSearch honours maxIterations (TreeSearch.searchGameTreeWithIterations, capped at 1000)
- CHANGED (FinancialData): YahooHistoryRequest gains StartDate and EndDate (end inclusive); the User-Agent is set per request instead of growing the client's default headers
- ADDED (chemistry): QPE.run performs real phase estimation of the molecular Hamiltonian (controlled Trotterised time evolution from the Hartree–Fock state, inverse QFT, peak refinement) instead of estimating one stand-in phase gate; H2 reaches −1.136546 Ha against FCI −1.137270 on 12 qubits. Results report every peak with its probability and the overlap caveat (EnergySource QpeTrotterEvolution, Estimation PhaseEstimation); the chemistry builder gains groundStateMethod QPE. UnifiedBackend circuit lowering is linear instead of quadratic
- CHANGED (chemistry): the whole-circuit UCCSD path's SPSA uses Spall gain rules and a shot-noise-based convergence test, refuses runs over ChemistryVQE.MaxWholeCircuitJobs (20,000 circuits) or the simulation time budget up front, honours cancellation, and never returns amplitudes worse than its start. The UCCSD ansatz is e^(T−T†) as documented (amplitude signs relative to earlier builds flip)
- CHANGED (cloud backends): every cloud backend (IonQ, Rigetti, Quantinuum, IQM, Atom Computing, Braket) transpiles circuits to its gate set before submission (GateTranspiler.transpileForBackendFully), so T, controlled-phase, Toffoli and multi-controlled gates reach the provider. Cloud backends no longer claim native algorithm intents they cannot run incrementally. Optional JobBudget limits the number of submitted jobs (unlimited by default)
- FIXED (Primitives): observe and expectation computed ⟨X⟩ and ⟨Y⟩ from phase-less cloud results and returned wrong values; on shot-sampling backends they now measure qubit-wise commuting groups in rotated bases (sampledExpectation, with standard errors). sample and run return the backend's own counts on shot-sampling backends instead of resampling them; a different requested shot count is an Error
- FIXED (ADAPT-VQE, ADAPT-QAOA): on shot-sampling backends both use sampled energies with parameter-shift gradients instead of finite differences that shot noise made meaningless
- CHANGED (QRNG): on shot-sampling backends the bits are the job's single measured shot (the backend must use shots = 1) instead of a classical pick among measured outcomes; one billed job per call
- FIXED (finance): QuantumMonteCarlo found the marked states from amplitude signs, which cloud results do not have (it returned 0 for a true 0.3); the marked set now comes from the oracle's definition and circuits take the whole-circuit route on cloud. Option pricing takes the price from the amplitude-estimation result with its standard error (it summed sampled bins classically), and the risk engine takes VaR, CVaR and volatility from amplitude estimation; added estimateBoundedExpectation
- CHANGED (chemistry): HamiltonianSimulation.simulateFromPreparation runs on whole-circuit backends and returns sampled probabilities; simulate on those backends returns an Error, since an arbitrary input state cannot be prepared there. The empirical-Hamiltonian VQE samples energies on whole-circuit backends
- CHANGED (QML): quantum-kernel matrices throttle concurrent jobs on shot-sampling backends and read P(0...0) from the job's counts without resampling
- CHANGED (whole-circuit routes): Shor, HHL, the quantum arithmetic operations and builder, AmplitudeAmplification, QFT.transformBasisState, Bell states, superdense coding, Bernstein–Vazirani, Deutsch–Jozsa, Simon, teleportation, BB84, E91 and the bit-flip and phase-flip codes now run on cloud backends: each keeps its native intent route (topological backend) and gate-by-gate route (simulator), and submits one whole circuit only when the backend refuses incremental operations (new Core/WholeCircuit helper; mid-circuit measurements deferred). Inputs other than |0...0⟩ and opaque custom oracles return an Error on those backends; the Shor 9-qubit and Steane code round trips return an Error there
- CHANGED (Shor): period-finding attempts are shots of one run, so a cloud backend gets one job. ShorsResult and PeriodFinderResult gain FactorSource; factors found by the gcd check are reported as classical preprocessing with no quantum period finding
- CHANGED (HHL): on whole-circuit backends the solution is read from counts as post-selected magnitudes (HHLResult.Readout); QuantumRegressionHHL returns an Error there, since regression weights need signs
- FIXED (Grover, tree search): the unknown-solution-count schedule starts at one Grover iteration, so results always involve amplification; tree search marks the sampled top percentile (its halved threshold marked most paths and made amplification worse than uniform) and uses explicit DefaultShots instead of a backend-name check
- KNOWN LIMITATION (fixed in 1.4.15): algorithms that call UnifiedBackend.measureState on a cloud result still draw their shots from its histogram with a pseudo-random generator

v1.4.12 (not published separately; included in 1.4.14): shots settings reach the circuit; network flow serves the most demand; QFT basis-state bit order

- CHANGED (different results): QuantumNetworkFlowSolver.solve returned the cheapest valid sample. Validity only bounds a flow from above, so a single supplier-to-customer path is valid and cheaper than serving everyone, and solve usually served part of the demand. It now returns the valid flow that meets the most demand, the cheapest among equals. NetworkFlow.solve, which calls it, changes the same way
- CHANGED (different results): QFT.transformBasisState (and encodeAndTransform) set bit i of the index on qubit i, while the QFT circuit reads qubit 0 as the most significant bit, so it transformed the bit-reversed index and matched neither reading of its documented formula. It now prepares |j⟩ in the circuit's convention, and the doc says where each amplitude lands: at the bit-reversed state-vector index with swaps, at k itself without. QFT.execute and executeOnState, and so QPE and Shor, are unchanged
- ADDED: QPE.executeWithShots takes the number of measurements, and QPE.DefaultShots (1000) names what executeWithExactness, executeWith and execute use. A non-positive count is a ValidationError
- CHANGED: the phaseEstimator builder's `shots` was stored and never used; QPE always measured 1000 times. estimate now measures `shots` times, or 1024 on LocalBackend and 2048 on any other backend when unset, as documented. `shots 0` is a ValidationError
- DOCS: the periodFinder and linearSystemSolver builders' `shots` is accepted but not used, and their docs now say so: Shor reads one measurement per attempt and retries (maxAttempts buys more samples), and the linear-system solver reads its answer from the state vector exactly
- CHANGED: the pattern matcher kept only outcomes above a fixed 5% frequency, which dropped every match of a broad pattern (128 matches in 256 items come out near 0.8% each). Candidates are verified classically anyway, so the threshold is now a uniform draw's frequency, 1/2^n
- CHANGED: circuit statistics counted measurements as single-qubit gates as well as measurements, so single + two + multi + measurements exceeded TotalGates. SingleQubitGates now excludes measurements

v1.4.11: GraphOptimization.validateConstraints checks what it claims; 2^n index lists removed from state-vector loops

- CHANGED (false passes): GraphOptimization.validateConstraints returned true for Acyclic without checking anything, and Connected only tested that the PROBLEM graph had at least one edge, so any solution passed both. Acyclic now detects a cycle (a directed cycle for directed edges; any cycle, including a self-loop or parallel edges, otherwise) and Connected checks that the selected edges form one component. Callers can now get false where they previously got true
- CHANGED: DegreeLimit and MinDegree judged the problem graph even when the solution selected edges, so a selection could break either and still pass. The structural constraints (DegreeLimit, MinDegree, Connected, Acyclic) now judge SelectedEdges when present and the problem graph otherwise. MinDegree also counts a node without edges as degree 0; iterating the adjacency map alone passed such nodes unchecked
- CHANGED (memory): Möttönen state preparation kept its rotation angles in a rectangular n x 2^(n-1) table, most of it unused, and at 20 qubits several times the memory of the state being prepared. It now keeps one array per level, 2^n - 1 angles in total, and its inner sums no longer allocate a throwaway list per control pattern. The emitted circuits are bit-identical: 23,360 gates across 1-10 qubits compared against v1.4.10
- CHANGED (memory): several state-vector loops walked the 2^n basis indices through a freshly built F# list, which for a 28-qubit state is several GB before any work starts: HHL's success probability, QuantumState.isNormalized, Measurement.getQubitProbabilities and computeExpectedValue, the QAOA simulator's expected cost and QuantumStateConversion.stateVectorToSparse. They are loops now, summing in the same order, so results are unchanged
- CHANGED: QuantumTspSolver's cost function turned ANY exception into a maximal tour-length penalty, so a decoding bug looked like a bad tour and the parameter optimiser steered around it silently. The catch-all is gone; QuantumTspSolver.solve already turns an exception into an Error, which is where such a bug now surfaces
- CHANGED: MaxCut.completeGraph, cycleGraph and pathGraph indexed their vertex list inside the edge loop (O(i) per access, cubic for the complete graph); they index an array now
- DOCS: the HHL success-probability doc described a condition-number correction that was never applied; the unused parameter is removed and the doc says it reports the measured probability. The four C# option-pricing helpers document numQubits, groverIterations and shots with their valid ranges, and no longer promise a "100x speedup" — the O(1/ε) versus O(1/ε²) advantage is asymptotic and assumes fault-tolerant hardware. Shor.execute's doc had drifted onto executeWith, which now has its own, including the exactness parameter

v1.4.10: Shor is quantum-only; local simulator capacity and speed

BREAKING CHANGES (vs 1.4.9) — the classical period-finding path is gone:
- REMOVED: Shor.PeriodFindingMethod, Shor.findPeriodWith, Shor.executeWithMethod, Shor.executeClassicallyAssisted, Shor.factorClassicallyAssisted, Shor.planPeriodFinding, Shor.ShorPeriodFindingIntent and Shor.ShorPeriodFindingPlan. ShorExecutionIntent loses its Method field and ShorPlan.ExecuteQuantum its method case field
- Why: that path found the period by trial division and then ran a QPE circuit whose phase was built FROM the period it had just found. It could not fail and it demonstrated nothing. Worse, it was not opt-in: Shor.execute asked for PeriodFindingMethod.Quantum and `plan` silently rewrote it to ClassicallyAssisted whenever the circuit did not fit the qubit budget, so callers received a classically computed factorization reported as Success with no field distinguishing it from a quantum one
- CHANGED: Shor.findPeriod and Shor.factor21 ran that path. They now run the genuine circuit — correct, and far slower. Shor.factor21 takes tens of minutes on a local simulator because factoring 21 on a simulated quantum computer takes tens of minutes
- CHANGED: N whose modular-exponentiation circuit exceeds the budget (N ≥ 33 or so at the default 20) now returns a ValidationError naming the shortfall, where it previously returned factors. Raise FSAQ_MAX_CIRCUIT_QUBITS to widen the budget
- CHANGED: Shor rejects QPE.Exactness.Approximate instead of accepting and ignoring it. Only the removed demonstration path ever read it; the quantum path builds an exact inverse QFT
- KEPT: the intent -&gt; plan -&gt; execution pipeline for period finding, which is what lets a non-simulator backend run Shor. Shor.ShorPeriodFindingIntent and Shor.ShorPeriodFindingPlan return with both cases quantum — ExecuteNatively (the backend claims modular-exponentiation QPE as one semantic intent) and ExecuteViaModExpCircuit (the Beauregard circuit, gate by gate). Shor.planPeriodFinding chooses between them per backend, and findPeriodQuantum plans once and reuses the plan across attempts. Previously this pipeline had a single case and it was the classical one, so the genuine path bypassed the abstraction entirely
- FIXED: LocalBackend.SupportsOperation returned true for EVERY QPE intent, including the modular-exponentiation unitary its own ApplyOperation refuses. Planning has to trust that answer, so it now reports by unitary — which is the capability question, whereas TargetQubits is a consequence of the unitary and a wrong count is malformed input rather than a missing capability
- NEW: Shor's period finding is a unified-backend algorithm. QPE.plan lowers a ModularExponentiation unitary to the Beauregard circuit instead of refusing it, so QPE.execute, QPE.executeWithExactness and QPE.plan all accept one where they previously returned "use Shor.estimateModExpPhase". Any gate backend runs the lowered gates; a braid backend compiles them to braids; a backend that claims the intent through SupportsOperation gets ExecuteNatively instead. Nothing needs a private copy of the arithmetic any more
- NEW: Algorithms.ModularExponentiationCircuit exposes that circuit as data — buildModExpQpe (the whole period-finding QPE), buildControlledModularMultiplication (C|y&gt; to C|a*y mod N&gt;), totalQubitsFor and workspaceQubits. It is extracted by running the existing Arithmetic implementation against a recording backend, which is sound because the modular arithmetic performs no measurement, reads no amplitude and branches on nothing but qubit indices and classical constants. There is therefore one implementation, not two, and the extracted circuit cannot drift from the executed one
- CHANGED: Algorithms/QuantumArithmetic.fs now compiles before QPE.fs (it references neither QFT nor QPE, so this is a pure reorder). That ordering was the only reason QPE could not lower modular exponentiation
- CHANGED: Shor.estimateModExpPhase builds its circuit from that same lowering instead of assembling Hadamards, eigenstate preparation, controlled multiplications and an inverse QFT inline. The two constructions agreed, but one circuit with two constructions is how they stop agreeing. Shor's register-width helper likewise delegates to ModularExponentiationCircuit.registerBitsFor, so the module cannot budget for a circuit of one size and then build one of another
- CHANGED: the lowered modular-exponentiation circuit emits its inverse QFT as an AlgorithmOperation.QFT intent instead of expanding it into H and controlled-phase gates. Expanding it dissolved the sub-algorithm into primitives that never reach a backend's intent dispatcher, so a backend able to transform the counting register directly was never asked — algorithm intents worked at the top of a call and then flattened. Backends without a native QFT lower it straight back to the same gates. The swaps stay explicit and appended, because the intent's inverse form applies them before the rotations while period finding needs them after
- FIXED (pre-existing): NoisyLocalBackend initialised as a StateVector while declaring NativeStateType = Mixed. UnifiedBackend.applySequence converts the initial state to the backend's native type before applying anything, and GateBased to Mixed is not implemented, so every applySequence-based algorithm (QPE, QFT, Grover) failed on it there — before the incremental-unsupported fallback that routes such backends to ExecuteToState could fire. It now initialises as |0..0&gt;&lt;0..0| in its own representation, and UnifiedBackend.isZeroState recognises a pure-zero density matrix so that fallback's from-|0&gt; check admits it. Single-qubit QPE now runs there end to end. Modular exponentiation still cannot: its Beauregard workspace alone is 2n + 4 qubits, over the density-matrix simulator's 8-qubit cap for any modulus, and it is now refused by that cap's ValidationError rather than by a thrown exception
- FIXED (crash): QPE.execute on a modular-exponentiation unitary THREW on every cloud backend and on the noisy local backend, instead of returning an error. Those backends refuse incremental ApplyOperation, so execution falls back to whole-circuit submission, and that fallback called the single-qubit lowering whose ModularExponentiation arm was a failwith justified as "plan() rejects it first" — an invariant that stopped holding the moment plan() learned to lower modular exponentiation. The same fallback also sized the state as counting + target, leaving the Beauregard circuit no workspace. The arm now returns an error, and plan, execute and the fallback all lower and size through one pair of helpers (lowerToOps, stateWidthFor) so they cannot drift apart again
- NEW: ModularExponentiationCircuit.buildModExpQpeAsGates — the same circuit as buildModExpQpe with its inverse QFT expanded to gates. Whole-circuit submission lowers an op list to a gate circuit and refuses any algorithm intent it meets, so it needs the gates spelled out; planners keep the intent form so a native QFT can compose
- CHANGED: QPE refuses Exactness.Approximate for modular exponentiation, as Shor already did. The lowering builds an exact inverse QFT and the native handlers transform exactly, so Approximate was accepted and silently ignored — the same input got two different answers depending on which entry point it went through
- CHANGED: QPE refuses a TargetQubits that disagrees with the modulus, and a custom EigenVector, for a ModularExponentiation unitary. The lowering derives the register width from the modulus and estimates the phase on |1&gt;, so both inputs were being silently ignored — a correct answer to a different question than the caller asked

- CHANGED: the local simulator's qubit ceiling is derived from available memory instead of a hardcoded 20. LocalSimulator.StateVector.maxQubits reports what the machine can actually hold, bounded at 30 (2^31 amplitudes exceed Array.MaxLength, so 30 is structural, not a policy). LocalBackend.MaxQubits follows it. Override with FSAQ_MAX_QUBITS; StateVector.maxQubitsForAvailableBytes lets a small machine compute what a larger one would allow
- NEW: StateVector.practicalCircuitQubits — a WALL-CLOCK budget (default 20, override FSAQ_MAX_CIRCUIT_QUBITS), deliberately separate from what fits in memory. Algorithms that choose between a genuine circuit and a classical fallback, such as Shor's modular exponentiation, budget against this one. The two limits are not interchangeable: a 26-qubit state fits comfortably and still takes hours to run, because every gate touches all 2^n amplitudes
- NEW: StateVector.stateVectorBytes for callers doing their own capacity planning
- FIXED (admitted work that could not finish): QuantumTspSolver, QaoaExecutionHelpers and ProblemDecomposition admitted a problem on memory capacity alone. Raising the qubit cap from a hardcoded 20 to a memory-derived 28-30 therefore widened what they accept: a 5-city TSP needs 25 qubits, which FITS and does not FINISH, so instead of a fast refusal it put a 25-qubit state through a thousand optimisation iterations. They now ask UnifiedBackend.getRunnableQubits
- NEW: BackendAbstraction.IWallClockLimitedBackend, with UnifiedBackend.getRunnableQubits taking it and MaxQubits together. MaxQubits answers "does it fit", PracticalQubits answers "will it finish", and admission needs both — conflating them is what the bug above was. Simulators implement it (LocalBackend reports StateVector.practicalCircuitQubits); hardware need not, since a shot costs the same there whatever the width. TaskScheduling.QuantumSolver had grown its own copy of this logic as a type test against LocalBackend and now uses the shared helper
- FIXED: the topological backends executed nothing for circuits submitted through any wrapper other than CircuitWrapper — ExecuteToState fell through to an empty result, so QAOA reported a cut of 0 instead of an error
- FIXED: Shor's outer factoring loop treated a period-finding failure as fatal. A base that yields no usable period is exactly what the retry budget exists for, and it now draws a fresh base rather than short-circuiting the run
- FIXED: QuantumTspSolver validates the backend's qubit capacity before building a circuit, and its optimizer iteration budget is configurable rather than fixed
- PERFORMANCE: measurement sampling builds the cumulative distribution once per call rather than once per shot — at 18 qubits and 1000 shots that was 1.9 s and ~2.9 GB of garbage, doubling with every added qubit
- PERFORMANCE: circuit execution applies gates in place instead of allocating a fresh 2^n array per gate, and the single-qubit kernel iterates amplitude pairs directly. Roughly 3.5x faster end to end on wide circuits

v1.4.9: Maintenance release — C# builder fix, property-test suite, formatting
- FIXED: the FSharp.Azure.Quantum.Business.CSharp.dll bundled in 1.4.8 was a stale build against the pre-1.4.8 reference unions, so C# builder calls that touch the now-[&lt;Struct&gt;] types could fail at runtime; it is rebuilt from the current sources. PredictiveModelBuilder (C#) reads ProblemType.Item directly — ProblemType is a struct union since 1.4.8 (the 1.4.8 note saying it stayed a reference union was wrong)
- NEW: tests/FSharp.Azure.Quantum.PropertyTests — FsCheck suite over random circuits: OpenQASM export/import round-trips in every version, exported text is a fixed point, comments never change a parse, invalid circuits are refused, angle expressions parse as written, damaged text yields Error not exceptions; GateTranspiler output for every backend and constraint set simulates to the same state up to global phase, uses only native gates, and is idempotent
- Codebase-wide Fantomas formatting; no functional changes intended

v1.4.8: Maintenance release — fsharp-refactor code clean-up (no functional changes intended)
- CHANGED: ~50 small config/result unions and records are now [&lt;Struct&gt;] (e.g. QFTConfig, NoiseModel, RetryConfig, ConfusionMatrix, PortfolioConfig, AtomCoord, Edge). Source-compatible; binary-incompatible — recompile consumers against 1.4.8
- CHANGED: OracleSpec.And/Or union fields are named (spec1, spec2)
- Ordinal string comparisons, invariant-culture number parsing, use-bindings for disposables, and narrower exception catches throughout
- ProblemType stays a reference union so C# callers can address its cases as nested types

v1.4.7: Correctness release — second-pass audit (silently-wrong-results fixes + dependency updates)

BREAKING CHANGES (vs 1.4.6):
- QuantumClientConfig gained a required Location field (Azure region, e.g. "eastus") and Client.createConfig takes it as a new parameter. QuantumClient now targets the {location}.quantum.azure.com DATA-PLANE endpoint — the previous management.azure.com (ARM) base URL made every live job call fail 401/404 against the quantum-scoped bearer token
- Amplitude encoding (FeatureMap.amplitudeEncoding) prepares the state with amplitude[i] = normalizedFeature[i] (state index = feature index). The previous construction was wrong for most inputs (skipped Gray-code CNOTs, wrong closing control, missing Gray reindexing of the multiplexed-RY angles) — kernels/VQC models trained on amplitude-encoded data should be retrained

FIXED (silently wrong results):
- CRX transpilation was the identity (X commutes with RX, so the RX-CNOT ladder cancelled) — now decomposed as H·CRZ(θ)·H; any transpiled circuit containing CRX was silently missing the gate
- Amplitude amplification's reflection about the prepared state executed A;S0;A† (operator A†·S0·A) instead of A†;S0;A — correct only for self-inverse preparations such as plain Grover's H⊗n
- HHL general (non-diagonal) path maps upper-half QPE register values to NEGATIVE eigenvalues (phase unwrap); a λ &lt; 0 component was previously inverted as phaseScale − |λ|. Phase scale is now 4·maxEig (positive and negative eigenvalues in disjoint phase halves; costs one bit of QPE precision)
- OpenQASM angle formatting in CircuitBuilder.toOpenQASM and QaoaCircuit.toOpenQasm uses InvariantCulture F10 (comma-decimal locales emitted "rx(1,57)" — invalid QASM submitted to paid hardware)
- QuantumClient.GetResultsAsync downloads results from outputDataUri (blob SAS URL) — the job model never inlines outputData, so the previous code could not return results
- SAS blob downloads (job results, Client + JobLifecycle) no longer carry the workspace bearer token: Azure Storage rejected it (401) and the token leaked to the storage host (AuthenticationHandler honors a per-request no-auth marker)
- VQC regression models persist ValueRange (ValueRangeMin/Max in SerializableVQCModel) — reloaded models previously produced affinely-rescaled predictions

FIXED (crashes / robustness):
- Rate-limit exponential backoff overflowed Int32 after ~23 consecutive 429s (Random.Next(min &gt; max) crashed the HTTP pipeline); backoff and job-poll delays now honor cancellation tokens
- Sync ExecuteToState wrappers on the five cloud backends run via Task.Run — no more permanent deadlock when called from a UI thread (WPF/WinForms)
- QAOA multi-start optimization no longer runs two extra objective evaluations (paid quantum jobs) per start for a log line, and only evaluates when a logger is attached
- HttpResponseMessage/JsonDocument disposal in QuantumClient and the D-Wave SAPI backend (socket exhaustion under load)
- Guards: correlation/covariance matrices over return series with no common dates; SMO training with a single sample; confusion matrix with test labels unseen at training; measurement standard deviation NaN from one-ulp negative variance; risk engine rejects SimulationPaths &lt; 2 with a validation error

UPDATED: FSharp.Core 10.1.302, Microsoft.Extensions.Logging 10.0.10, OpenTelemetry 1.17.0, Serilog 4.4.0

v1.4.6: Correctness release — full-library audit (25 critical + ~35 high-severity fixes)

BREAKING CHANGES (vs 1.4.5):
- submitAndWaitForResultsAsync takes a trailing CancellationToken on IonQ/Rigetti/Quantinuum/AtomComputing/Pasqal backends (polling is now cancellable)
- QaoaOptimizer.Optimizer.minimizeWithBounds takes tolerance and maxIterations parameters (config values are now honored; non-convergence returns best-so-far instead of throwing)
- QuantumState gained a MeasurementHistogram case (and QuantumStateType.Sampled) — exhaustive matches must handle or wildcard it
- MitigationTechnique.ReadoutErrorMitigation payload is now CalibrationMatrix option (no fabricated default calibration; missing calibration = pass-through)
- QuantumGraphColoringSolver.solveClassical returns Result instead of throwing when colors run out
- constraintSolver's searchSpace now means NUMBER OF VARIABLES (matching forSudokuStyle/forNQueens); numVariables × log2(domainSize) ≤ 16 qubits is enforced with a clear error
- Knapsack/set-cover/drug-discovery QUBOs use slack variables for inequality constraints (NumVariables grew; decoders unchanged for item bits)
- findCheapestBackend/recommendCostOptimization exclude subscription-priced backends (Quantinuum has no per-job USD price); an all-subscription backend list returns an Error / no recommendation
- Business classification/regression builders REJECT feature counts above the qubit cap (8 binary / 5 predictive) with a clear error instead of failing every epoch (1.4.5) or silently truncating; reduce dimensionality first
- MitigatedResult gained CorrectionApplied: false means the histogram passed through uncorrected (readout mitigation selected without calibration data)

NEW: Wide cloud hardware support
- QuantumState.MeasurementHistogram: sampled results with NO qubit-count limit (at most `shots` entries regardless of width)
- Cloud result tiering everywhere (Braket + Azure backends): dense state ≤ 20 qubits, sparse state 21-31, measurement histogram beyond — Quantinuum H2 (56q), Rigetti Ankaa (~84q), IonQ Forte (36q) results are now usable (previously crashed above 20)

HIGH-severity fixes (selection):
- IonQ result parser accepts the documented Azure probability-histogram format (decimal state keys); live jobs no longer error
- OpenQASM export defines rxx/ryy/rzz inline where qelib1/stdgates lack them; importer accepts the definitions
- VQC gradients apply the chain rule (parameter-shift on circuit expectations × analytic loss derivative) — was a biased finite difference of the loss
- VQC model serialization stores PauliFeatureMap/TwoLocal configuration losslessly (saveVQCModelWithConfigAsync); AutoML train/validation split is seeded-shuffled
- Parametric VaR honors the requested confidence for Student-t/log-normal; Historical redirects to the empirical function; Asian options priced via the exact geometric-average distribution (was silently European)
- Network-flow conservation penalty de-doubled and solutions validated (conservation/supply/demand) before selection; graph-coloring fixed colors enforced; scheduling objective (incl. MinimizeLateness) actually honored
- Portfolio: negative-return zero-risk assets no longer rank first; quantum decode respects Min/MaxHolding; KnapsackBuilder solveClassicalDP is genuine dynamic programming
- Shor draws a fresh random base per retry (N=33-style permanent failures fixed; gcd hits return the factor immediately)
- QRNG local path uses a cryptographic RNG and documentation states it is CSPRNG, not quantum, randomness
- Molecular CSV labels stay aligned; unlabeled drug screening errors instead of reporting every candidate as a hit; malformed SMILES fail at parse time
- Cost recommendations exclude subscription-priced backends (Quantinuum HQC no longer 'cheapest' at $0); QAOA optimizer non-convergence returns best-so-far
- Density-matrix simulator handles Reset/Barrier/Conditional with exact channels; strategy selection no longer allocates a 2^n×2^n placeholder (34 GB at 16 qubits)

v1.4.6 critical fixes (earlier batch):
- FIXED: Circuit adjoint (CircuitBuilder.reverse) now reverses gate order — corrects amplitude amplification reflections and quantum Monte Carlo Grover operators
- FIXED: Multi-controlled X/Z decomposition for 3+ controls (correct ancilla-free recursion; the old "borrowed ancilla" ladder computed the wrong predicate) — affects Grover 4+ qubits and HHL eigenvalue inversion
- FIXED: QUBO -&gt; Ising conversion (fromQubo/fromQuboSparse) now includes cross-term linear contributions, and QuboExtraction is its exact inverse (D-Wave path optimized a corrupted objective before)
- FIXED: Trotter-Suzuki Pauli decomposition qubit ordering (was bit-reversed vs circuit synthesis) and diagonal decomposition (correct I/Z expansion via Walsh-Hadamard transform); HHL controlled-evolution gate order; controlled evolution of identity terms emits the control phase
- FIXED: Zero-noise extrapolation scaled circuits executed in reverse gate order
- FIXED: Budget checks no longer skip the monthly limit when a daily warning fires
- FIXED: Task scheduling — resource capacity encoded with non-negative overload penalties (equality penalty rewarded infeasible schedules); decoded schedules re-validated classically (one-hot, dependencies, resource capacity) with earliest-slot one-hot repair
- FIXED: Grover constraint solver — searchSpace is the number of variables (matching forSudokuStyle/forNQueens), with a 16-qubit feasibility guard; mixed-radix decode no longer overflows
- FIXED: Graph coloring greedy solver returns Result instead of throwing when colors run out (HybridSolver propagates)
- FIXED: ML — amplitude-encoding Walsh-Hadamard butterfly (wrong state for 3+ qubits), 2-class VQC prediction (always returned first class), anomaly detection rebuilt on kernel centroid distance (one-class SVM was degenerate and could never learn); model loaders validate model kind (wrong-kind files no longer load as VQC-regression with null parameters)
- FIXED: Invariant-culture number parsing in chemistry and market data (comma-decimal locales silently lost elements/atoms/returns)
- FIXED: Parametric Expected Shortfall no longer overstated by factor z (~2.33x at 99%); risk engine errors on missing/malformed data instead of fabricating zeros or mock returns
- FIXED: D-Wave direct backend speaks the documented SAPI v2 qp wire format (base64 lin/quad against solver topology; correct submit/poll/answer decoding); problems off the hardware graph error explicitly (minor-embedding not implemented)
- NOTE: P(theta) transpiles to RZ(theta) on backends without a native phase gate (equal up to global phase)

v1.4.3: Neutral-atom analog mode, CUDA-Q-style primitives, and new backends
- NEW: Neutral-atom (Rydberg) analog programming - NeutralAtom module Trotterizes an analog program to gates; PasqalBackend (RydbergProgram -&gt; Pulser JSON -&gt; Azure Quantum) and QueraBackend (RydbergProgram -&gt; Braket AHS JSON)
- NEW: CUDA-Q-style execution primitives (Primitives: sample/observe/run/getState) over IQuantumBackend, plus CudaQBridge
- NEW: ADAPT-VQE and ADAPT-QAOA adaptive-ansatz algorithms (AdaptVqe, AdaptQaoa)
- NEW: IQM gate backend (IqmBackend)
- NEW: Density-matrix noisy simulator backend (NoisyBackend)
- NEW: Emulate-target mode (Emulation) - transpile, validate, then run locally for a cloud target
- NEW: Companion FSharp.Azure.Quantum.Braket package isolates the AWS SDK dependency (QuEra neutral-atom analog and Braket gate QPUs)
- IMPROVED: Business builders and ML model serialization refactors

v1.4.2: Compilation/hardware tooling and weighted MAX-SAT
- NEW: QubitRouting - inserts SWAPs so two-qubit gates respect device connectivity (CouplingMap: grid/linear/fromPairs); tracks logical-to-physical qubit permutation
- NEW: CloudBackends.Factory.createRigettiRouted - Rigetti backend that auto-routes circuits for a device coupling map
- NEW: QirEmitter - emit circuits as QIR base-profile textual LLVM IR for Azure Quantum submission
- NEW: ResourceEstimation - logical (qubits/gates/T-count/depth) and physical (surface-code) resource estimates
- NEW: NoiseModel - DeviceNoiseProfile with noise-aware routing (routeNoiseAware) and success-probability estimation
- NEW: Weighted MAX-SAT - per-clause weights in QuantumSatSolver (clause / weightedClause); solution reports SatisfiedWeight/TotalWeight
- IMPROVED: AutoML returns a typed TrainedModel discriminated union instead of obj (no unboxing in predict)
- IMPROVED: Connected-component decomposition now unions sub-solutions for Vertex Cover and Maximum Matching
- IMPROVED: Knapsack builder shares the solver item type (no duplicate record or field mapping)

v1.4.1: Dependency updates
- UPDATED: Azure.Identity 1.13.1 to 1.21.0
- UPDATED: FSharp.Core to 10.1.301
- UPDATED: Microsoft.Extensions.Logging to 10.0.9, Serilog to 4.3.1
- UPDATED: OpenTelemetry to 1.16.0 (Exporter.Console 1.16.0, Instrumentation.Http 1.15.1)

v1.3.10: Async backend refactoring
- NEW: Task-based async APIs on IQuantumBackend (ExecuteToStateAsync, ApplyOperationAsync) with CancellationToken
- NEW: Async QAOA execution helpers (executeQaoaCircuitAsync, executeQaoaWithGridSearchAsync, executeWithBudgetAsync)
- NEW: Async ML pipeline (computeKernelAsync, computeKernelMatrixAsync, predictAsync, evaluateAsync)
- NEW: Cloud backend factory (CloudBackendFactory) for Rigetti, IonQ, Quantinuum, AtomComputing
- NEW: UnifiedBackend async helpers (applySequenceAsync, applyWithConversionAsync)
- NEW: OpenQASM export async (exportToFileAsync, exportToFileWithConfigAsync)
- IMPROVED: All async methods use task {} CE (not async {}), returning Task&lt;T&gt; for .NET interop
- IMPROVED: Sync methods marked [Obsolete] where async alternatives exist
- FIXED: 6 pre-existing .fsx script failures (MathNet dependency, syntax errors)
- Status: Production-ready</PackageReleaseNotes>

